(m_g/m_i)_1.description:
Represents the ratio of gravitational mass (

\(m_g\)

) to inertial mass (

\(m_i\)

) for the first test body or material. In the WEP, this ratio is universally constant, typically unity.

(m_g/m_i)_2.description:
Represents the ratio of gravitational mass (

\(m_g\)

) to inertial mass (

\(m_i\)

) for the second test body or material. This ratio is compared against that of the first body to test the WEP.

charge-consistency-c2c1ad37.conceptual_definition:
This formula quantifies the degree to which different materials adhere to the Weak Equivalence Principle (WEP). The WEP is a fundamental concept in physics stating that the gravitational mass and inertial mass of an object are equivalent, implying that all objects fall with the same acceleration in a given gravitational field, regardless of their composition or internal structure. The formula calculates a dimensionless parameter, the Eötvös parameter (

\(\eta\)

), which measures the fractional difference between the ratios of gravitational mass to inertial mass for two different test bodies.

\\Delta Q_{heat}.description:
Represents the amount of thermal energy transferred between the system and its surroundings due to a temperature difference. In this specific formulation, a positive value for 
\Delta Q_{heat}
 indicates that heat is lost from the system to the surroundings. If heat is gained by the system, this value would be negative.

W_{ext}.description:
Represents the work done on the system by external forces or pressures. This work involves organized energy transfer due to macroscopic forces acting over a distance. A positive value for 
W_{ext}
 means work is done on the system by its surroundings, increasing its internal energy. If the system performs work on its surroundings, this value would be negative.

right-hand-check-98eb04c1.interpretation:
This equation, 
\Delta U_{system} + \Delta Q_{heat} = W_{ext}
, represents a specific formulation of the First Law of Thermodynamics. It states that the change in the internal energy of a system, 
\Delta U_{system}
, plus the heat lost by the system to its surroundings, 
\Delta Q_{heat}
, is equal to the work done on the system by external forces, 
W_{ext}
. In this convention: 
\Delta U_{system}
 is positive if the internal energy of the system increases. 
\Delta Q_{heat}
 is positive if heat flows out of the system (i.e., heat is lost by the system). If heat flows into the system, 
\Delta Q_{heat}
 would be negative. 
W_{ext}
 is positive if work is done on the system by its surroundings. If the system does work on its surroundings, 
W_{ext}
 would be negative. Thus, the equation can be rearranged as 
\Delta U_{system} = W_{ext} - \Delta Q_{heat}
, which means the change in internal energy is the work done on the system minus the heat lost by the system, or equivalently, the work done on the system plus the heat gained by the system.
